snapshot08/10/88This program gives users of serial terminals some of the capability ofthe PC's PrtSc key.Currently it supports the Wyse 50 and Heath/Zenith 29 terminals.  Ithink that the H29 code also works on H19's.  The Wyse 50 code onlyworks in 80-column mode; I experimented with the extended cursorpositioning that supports the 132-column mode, but I couldn't get itto work.Installing Terminal DefinitionsTermcap/terminfo are not used because it would be difficult to coercethem into dealing with the oddities of producing a screen dump.However, snapshot is easily extendable, by recompiling, for anyterminal that can transmit the contents of the screen.Issues that must be dealt with include: - where must the cursor be in order to transmit a line?  (probably   either at the start or end of the line) - what is the code to tell the terminal to transmit a line? - in what format does it transmit the line?  (does it include special   escape sequences?) - how is the cursor position saved/restored?  (might be defined as the    sc and rc termcap capabilities.  if the terminal can't save/restore   the cursor position, you can try using its "return cursor   coordinates" code; I had trouble reading the Wyse 50's cursor   position, so I #ifdef'd that code out and just have it return to the   bottom of the screen every time)Here's what you need to do:1.  Create a file similar to wy50.c and h29.c that definesname_savepos(), name_restorepos(), and name_transline() for the newterminal.  Transmitting a whole screenful at once has been unreliablein my experience, and it precludes specifying starting and ending lines;but if you want to do it, you can have name_transline() keep a staticvariable and be a no-op all but the first time it is called.2.  Add function declarations and an entry in tdtab[], and incrementNTD, in main.c3.  Add the file from step 1. to the Makefile and remake the program.If you can figure out a better way to support different terminals, feelfree to change things around!The program is designed modularly so that it is easy to replace parts ofit, if necessary (e.g. for porting to other OS's), and its functionsclean up after themselves so they could be linked in as parts of otherprograms.David MacKenzieedf@rocky2.rockefeller.edu (...rutgers!cmcl2!rocky2!edf).